---
Run these commands from the ZML checkout used in the
[quickstart]($link.page('zml/quickstart')). Bazel selects and builds the target.
Each example executable defines its runtime arguments.

# [Build, run, or test a target]($heading.id('build-run-or-test-a-target'))

Use `bazel query '//examples/...'` to list example targets.
`bazel build //examples/mnist` builds MNIST. `bazel run //examples/mnist`
also launches it with its test data. Run the framework tests with
`bazel test //zml:test`. See [Build and run with Bazel]($link.page('zml/build-and-run').ref('build-run-or-test-a-target'))
for the target workflow.

# [Choose a backend and target platform]($heading.id('choose-a-backend-and-target-platform'))

The backend flags select the runtimes compiled into the executable.
`--platforms` selects the target operating system and architecture.
Choose your hardware in the [MNIST quickstart]($link.page('zml/quickstart').ref('build-and-run-mnist'))
to get a complete command. The [backend flags and defaults]($link.page('zml/build-and-run').ref('choose-a-backend-and-target-platform'))
cover cross-builds and runtime selection.

# [Run an LLM example]($heading.id('run-an-llm-example'))

Place Bazel options before the target. Use `--` after the target to pass the
remaining arguments to the executable. The example lists its own options
when called with `--help`.

```sh
bazel run //examples/llm -- --help
```

Set `--model` to the checkpoint and `--prompt` for a single response.
See [LLM example flags and defaults]($link.page('zml/build-and-run').ref('run-an-llm-example'))
for the accepted checkpoint locations and attention backends.
